WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:05.680
Now that you know the steps to take to organize&nbsp;
and plan a database, let’s get to building one.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.680 --> 00:00:13.200
We are going to be using Structured Query Language&nbsp;
(SQL) to build our database. To start this domain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.200 --> 00:00:17.760
on database object management, I want to start&nbsp;
with adding tables to a database. When we create&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.760 --> 00:00:23.520
tables, we want to specify three things: The&nbsp;
table's name. The columns to add to the table,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.520 --> 00:00:28.640
and The data types for each column within the&nbsp;
table. Notice this CREATE statement for creating&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.640 --> 00:00:39.600
a table. It contains the table's (entity's) name,&nbsp;
the columns to use, and the data type for each&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.640 --> 00:00:46.800
column. I also want you to notice the naming&nbsp;
convention we are using here. There are no spaces&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.440 --> 00:00:52.720
in the names of any fields. In SQL, spaces are&nbsp;
not allowed in object names unless those object&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.720 --> 00:00:59.440
names are in square brackets. For consistency's&nbsp;
sake, it's best not to have spaces in field names.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.080 --> 00:01:06.560
Also, SQL is not case-sensitive by nature, but&nbsp;
many programming languages that use SQL statements&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.120 --> 00:01:14.560
are case-sensitive. Thus, it is important to have&nbsp;
consistency in casing among column names, as here,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.560 --> 00:01:21.280
we see each word in each column name starts&nbsp;
with a capital letter. I will run this code,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.040 --> 00:01:30.160
and we have our new table. Once a table is&nbsp;
created, the only way to change it is to alter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.160 --> 00:01:35.040
the table. We never run a CREATE statement&nbsp;
again when trying to make changes to an entity.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.040 --> 00:01:40.480
We, instead, as we see in this file,&nbsp;
alter a table using the ALTER command.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.200 --> 00:01:45.600
Notice that only one alteration&nbsp;
is allowed per ALTER statement.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.600 --> 00:01:55.520
The first adds a column to a table, but the word&nbsp;
column is not specified for an ADD statement.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.520 --> 00:02:00.880
The second alters an existing column to change&nbsp;
the number of characters allowed in the column&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.440 --> 00:02:06.480
and switches this to nvarchar as the database&nbsp;
users want to use symbols when needed in their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.480 --> 00:02:14.000
descriptions. You will also notice the DROP TABLE&nbsp;
command in the same query. Having multiple SQL&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.000 --> 00:02:18.400
statements in one window is fine because we can&nbsp;
select lines to execute and then select Execute&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.400 --> 00:02:24.480
to run the code for just the selected lines. I&nbsp;
will select the ALTER statements and run those,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.800 --> 00:02:33.280
and we see a successful result. The DROP TABLE&nbsp;
command removes a table from a database. Of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.280 --> 00:02:37.760
course, we need to be very careful with this&nbsp;
because dropping a table also deletes everything&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.760 --> 00:02:45.040
within that table. I will select and then run&nbsp;
this DROP TABLE statement to drop this test table,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.760 --> 00:02:55.200
and the table has been removed from the&nbsp;
database. I will refresh the list of objects&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.200 --> 00:03:03.840
within this server to prove this,&nbsp;
and the test table is now gone.

